data_curation:
All data are self-generated or drawn from open sets. SFT data: OpenR1-Math queries; the LRM itself samples trajectories under two forced templates, SHORT (<think>

</think>

) and LONG (<think>
), and only template-valid, exact-answer-correct samples are kept. About 20K per mode (40K total) are used for full-parameter SFT and 4K per mode for LoRA, with no requirement that a problem be correct in both modes. R1-Distill models lack a no-think mode, so they take original OpenR1 solutions for LONG and Qwen2-Math-72B-Instruct distillations for SHORT, with instructional prefixes marking the mode. RPO data: ~1K queries from part of s1k plus a compression dataset. Items with LONG accuracy <0.25 are dropped, and the rest are stratified 1:1 into easy (acc_S >= 0.75) and hard (acc_S < acc_L <= 0.5).

method:
RPO (Root-token Policy Optimization) treats adaptive thinking as a single categorical routing action at the generation root: z in {
 (LONG), 

 (SHORT)} right after <think>. Each prompt gets n=16 balanced rollouts (8 forced SHORT, 8 forced LONG; temperature 0.6, top-p 0.95, 16K context). Each rollout's reward is its mode group's correct count, r = alpha_m * C_m(x), with alpha_L = 1 and a mild SHORT preference alpha_S = 1 + 1/n. Advantages are standardized per mode, pooled across the batch, because the within-prompt mode-group variance is zero. Only log p(z|x) gets gradients (all other tokens masked), with KL to the post-SFT reference. Routing at inference thresholds p_short(x) >= tau. Implementation uses OpenRLHF with an async rollout engine, batch 128, lr 1e-6, 3 H100s. Peak VRAM for a 7B model at 13K context falls to 0.36x of full GRPO (0.17x with LoRA).
